<article class="env-lemma" id="0AQJ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0AQJ">Lemma <span data-tag="0AQJ">88.3.6</span>.</a> With assumptions as in Lemma <a href="/tag/0ALM" data-tag="0ALM">88.3.5</a> assume that $B/I^ nB \to C/I^ nC$ is a local complete intersection homomorphism for all $n$. Then $H^{-1}(\mathop{N\! L}\nolimits _{B/A}^\wedge \otimes _ B C) \to H^{-1}(\mathop{N\! L}\nolimits _{C/A}^\wedge )$ is injective. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      For each $n \geq 1$ we set $A_ n = A/I^ n$, $B_ n = B/I^ nB$, and $C_ n = C/I^ nC$. We have 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \begin{align*}  H^{-1}(\mathop{N\! L}\nolimits _{B/A}^\wedge \otimes _ B C) &  = \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits H^{-1}(\mathop{N\! L}\nolimits _{B/A}^\wedge \otimes _ B C_ n) \\ &  = \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits H^{-1}(\mathop{N\! L}\nolimits _{B/A}^\wedge \otimes _ B B_ n \otimes _{B_ n} C_ n) \\ &  = \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits H^{-1}(\mathop{N\! L}\nolimits _{B_ n/A_ n} \otimes _{B_ n} C_ n) \end{align*}
</div>
    <p>
       The first equality follows from More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0EGU" data-tag="0EGU">15.102.1</a> and the fact that $H^{-1}(\mathop{N\! L}\nolimits _{B/A}^\wedge \otimes _ B C)$ is a finite $C$-module and hence $I$-adically complete for example by Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00MA" data-tag="00MA">10.97.1</a>. The second equality is trivial. The third holds by Lemma <a href="/tag/0AJS" data-tag="0AJS">88.3.3</a>. The maps $H^{-1}(\mathop{N\! L}\nolimits _{B_ n/A_ n} \otimes _{B_ n} C_ n) \to H^{-1}(\mathop{N\! L}\nolimits _{C_ n/A_ n})$ are injective by More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/07D4" data-tag="07D4">15.34.6</a>. The proof is finished because we also have $H^{-1}(\mathop{N\! L}\nolimits _{C/A}^\wedge ) = \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits H^{-1}(\mathop{N\! L}\nolimits _{C_ n/A_ n})$ similarly to the above. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>